       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 9,000 mg/day of acetaminophen is more than twice the 4 g/day maximum. A 70 kg patient with normal liver function would be at real risk of acute liver injury, and I can't think of a clinical scenario that justifies it. Entering it as written could seriously harm the patient.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose right away.** For example, 1000 mg PO three times daily (3 g/day) or 1000 mg PO every 6 hours (4 g/day).\n- **Check the intent.** If the goal is stronger analgesia, I can enter a scheduled acetaminophen dose at or below the maximum. You would add another agent, which you'd choose.\n- **Escalate if you still believe this dose is intended.** Talk to your attending and pharmacy. If they approve it, they can enter or override it through your hospital's high-dose verification process.\n\nTell me which standard regimen you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
